<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Habuba Kabira</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Habuba Kabira is an archaeological site on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, now flooded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-assad">Lake Assad</a> behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabqa-dam">Tabqa Dam</a>. Excavated in the late 1960s and 1970s, it revealed a planned, walled town of the late fourth millennium BCE with southern Mesopotamian architecture, pottery and administrative tools, the best-known colony of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> expansion.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Habuba Kabira lies on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in Aleppo Governorate, about 1,300 km from the southern Mesopotamian city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, 5 km north of Tell Halawa and 10 km south of Mumbaqat; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-aruda">Jebel Aruda</a> lies 8 km upstream. The complex has two sites. Habuba Kabira South is a flat Lower Town with the acropolis of Tell Qanas as its Upper Town. Habuba Kabira North is a high mound about 230 meters across, rising about 14 meters above the plain, which was occupied from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk-period">Uruk period</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Habuba Kabira South was built in the later 4th millennium BCE as part of the Uruk Expansion and was abandoned after about 120 years. It was a planned town with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mudbrick</a> wall three meters wide, regular towers and two gates, laid out along a north-south axis with paved streets and drains. A walled area at its centre held cultic and administrative buildings, and an irrigated farming area lay to the south. The first settlement, on virgin soil, covered about 6 hectares; at its largest, with houses outside the wall, the town covered 22 hectares.</p><p>On Tell Qanas stood the tripartite North Temple and South Temple and a large administrative building with a storeroom of jars, many sealed with cylinder seals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clay-tokens">Clay tokens</a> and three numerical tablets of the Uruk V period were found. As at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-aruda">Jebel Aruda</a> and similar sites, no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk-period">Uruk period</a> burials came to light. Other late Uruk outposts of the region include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arslantepe">Arslantepe</a>, Tell Sheikh Hassan and Hacınebi Tepe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site was first recorded in surveys of 1963 and 1964 by Abdel Kader Rihaoui and Maurits N. van <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borgloon">Loon</a>. A Belgian team under André Finet excavated Tell Qanas from 1967, and a Deutsche Orient-Gesellschaft team under Ernst Heinrich and Eva Strommenger worked for 9 seasons from 1969 to 1975, as rescue archaeology before the building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabqa-dam">Tabqa Dam</a>. The site is now mostly under water.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Habuba Kabira” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The city depended on materials it lacked, such as timber, stone and metals, and in the so-called Uruk expansion settlers founded outposts and colonies far up the Euphrates in Syria and south-eastern Anatolia, such as <b>Habuba Kabira</b> and Jebel Aruda, and in western Iran at Godin Tepe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
